**Better question first.** Rather than "which activities depend on which?", ask: **at each handover, what must be *vacated* — not merely finished — and who is waiting on a person rather than on equipment?** That is: (1) which single-instance things (each of the two ergs, the coach's attention) must be free before the next body can use them; (2) for each rower, is the next step pinned to the session clock, to their own previous piece, or to *another pair stepping off a machine*; and (3) is a gap a real activity or just the handover interval?

Answered: no erg is ever mounted while someone who isn't rowing is on it, so each pair's piece chain hangs off its own previous piece (A/B) or off the other pair's piece ending (C/D); A/B's 4-minute gap lands exactly on C/D coming off, so it is a buffer, not a step. The coach is one serial resource: 5-minute board brief before anyone mounts, 20 minutes of split-calling starting at the *start* of the first piece, 12 minutes of one-at-a-time drills only once the last erg piece is off, and a 10-minute debrief after the cooldown. The floor cooldown waits for the last drill. Nothing new is inferred — the text's own note says the gaps and the off-pair's "rest" are handovers, and an erg bay needs no preheat/thaw/cool hold.

